A||||7||85fierogt||how in the........do i get those out of there???||||5||
Z||000000||85fierogt||10-16-2001||05:44 PM||||ok how do i get the rear e-brake cables out of the frame, if u have no idea what im talking about i will calrify, im stumped, im doin a full e-brake cable replacement,<BR>thanks<BR>-peace<BR>-brad||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mickey_Moose||10-16-2001||06:04 PM||||never done this myself...but could you not just pull the cable from the one end and pull it through? I would tie a small rope on the end, before pulling it through, it would make it easier to pull the new cable through by attaching it to the rope and then pull on the rope.<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||85fierogt||10-16-2001||06:13 PM||||well no actually because when u insert a cable into the frame it has sumtin like 4 or 6 prongs that make it very ery difficult to remove and me being the lucky one that has to im wondering how since i cannot get to them because they are inserted into the frame.grrrrrrrrrr, thanks though for trying i do appreciate it<BR>-peace<BR>-brad||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroSoul||10-16-2001||06:41 PM||||  Just did my right side about 2 weeks ago. On the frame at one end of the cable, there is an oval shaped opening above and slightly in front of where the cable goes through the frame.  Use a screwdriver in the opening to depress the prongs as you push the cable through towards the center of the car.  For the other end of the cable, you have to take the spring off first and then squeeze the prongs in with pliers.  Needle nose pliers might work inside the frame also if they are long enough.  Hope this helps.  Not as hard as it looks.||||reg||6
Z||000004||Phil||10-16-2001||06:48 PM||||You say you're doing a cable replacement, if so just mangle and clip off the little ears with a pair of cutters or just grab and yank like heck.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jncomutt||10-16-2001||09:09 PM||||There were 3 of the little hooks on mine.  I pried one up with a little flathead until it broke.  Then turned the cable, pushed that one down.  Turned the cable over, and pushed down the other pin, slid right out.  Instalation is as easy as a push and a click.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||10-17-2001||07:31 PM||||Small hose clamp tightened just enough to release the cable ears. Metal tubing of the correct diameter works too. Or just break them off if the cable is junk.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000007||West Coast Fiero||10-17-2001||08:41 PM||||slip the closed end of a 13mm wrench over the tangs and slightly wobble the wrench until the cable housing releases from the frame - has always worked for me<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
